body:
There are a ton of factors to consider when planning your next steps. You could try and identify the playstyle you'd like the best in the endgame and aim to pick up the classes that go with that. It's hard to know what you'll like before you're even there, but eventually you'll pick up everything anyways so you won't be pigeonholed into one choice if you change your mind.

&#x200B;

Nekromancer -> Omnimancer -> Heretic: The playstyle will continue to be similar to Druid. You've got lots of skill slots, mana, and tons of different elemental spells to equip for any situation. And you've got the powerful non-elemental Summon Dead nukes, for which the melee classes don't really have a counterpart. I picked the mage path and it's what I'm most familiar with. I really enjoy it!

&#x200B;

Atlas Vanguard -> Titanguard -> Gilgamesh: A focus on defense, maintaining high Ward to tank hits and regain mana. I think this is currently considered the best option for surviving against the very hardest content.

&#x200B;

Arcanic -> Nyx -> Realmshifter: A focus on damage, balancing less defense for more evasiveness and healing. I'm least familiar with this, but it does look fun with its explosive gameplay.

&#x200B;

Freyr -> Bahamut -> Beowulf: Trading less character power for a stronger follower. More flexible gear choices, and you can opt to do melee, magic, or a mix (though the class passives are a little more melee focused). Currently stupid good in 9\* pvp, but considered weak in 10\* content.

&#x200B;

Faction gods -> Deity: More well-rounded stats, tons of skill slots, and can wear any equipment. Can do pretty much any playstyle, but not focused as specifically as the other classes.
